+++ b/README.md
@@ -17,10 +17,18 @@ The function will inject the scope, node and environment data into the debugger
 allowing you to poke around to see variables, functions, facts, classes, and resources defined in the current scope.
 
 ## Requirements
-Ensure you have installed the puppet-debugger gem `gem install puppet-debugger`
-or place this in your Gemfile `gem 'puppet-debugger', '>= 0.4'` for your puppet module.
+Puppet 7 or newer, or OpenVox 8, and the puppet-debugger gem installed for the Ruby that runs
+your Puppet code. With a system Ruby: `gem install puppet-debugger`, or add
+`gem 'puppet-debugger'` to your module's Gemfile. With the OpenVox agent or OpenBolt packages,
+use their own `gem` command and the RubyGems user directory, which their wrappers keep on the
+gem path:
 
-This also requires puppet 3.8+ with future parser enabled.
+    /opt/puppetlabs/puppet/bin/gem install --user-install puppet-debugger
+    /opt/puppetlabs/bolt/bin/gem install --user-install puppet-debugger
+
+On an isolated network copy the `.gem` file across and add `--local`. The gem has no
+dependencies, so that one file is all that is needed. If the gem is missing,
+`debug::break()` fails with the install command for the Ruby in use.
 
 You will also want to include this module in your fixtures file if using for rspec-puppet
 unit testing.
@@ -99,6 +107,11 @@ Type "exit", "functions", "vars", "krt", "whereami", "facts", "resources", "clas
 
 If using with rspec-puppet, only the facts you define in your test suite will be present in the debugger.
 
+### Bolt plans
+`debug::break()` also works inside Bolt plans. Add the module to the project's `Puppetfile` or
+`modules/` directory and call the function where you want to stop; Bolt's `$targets`, results and
+plan variables are then available in the session, and Bolt objects print as their data.
+
 For more information on how to use the puppet debugger please refer to the [documentation](https://github.com/nwops/puppet-debugger)
 
 ## Troubleshooting
